 Nested Loops in C — Pattern Programs

Note: In nested loops, the -outer loop represents the number of 'rows', 
  and the -inner loop represents the number of 'columns'.


EXAMPLES

 1. Number / Character / Star Triangle Patterns

Prints one of three variants depending on which printf line is uncommented:


1                a                *
1 2              a b              * *
1 2 3            a b c            * * *
1 2 3 4          a b c d          * * * *


#include<stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int i, j;
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        for (j = 0; j <= i; j++)
        {
            // printf("%c", 'a' + j);   // a b c d e
            // printf("%d", 1 + j);     // 1 2 3 4 5
            // printf("* ");            // * * * *
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}


How it builds (rows vs columns):

| Row | i value | Columns printed |

| 1st | i = 0   | 1 time  |
| 2nd | i = 1   | 2 times |
| 3rd | i = 2   | 3 times |
| 4th | i = 3   | 4 times |
| 5th | i = 4   | 5 times |



 2. Alternating Numbers / Letters Based on Row Parity

Prints a letter on even rows and a number on odd rows:


1
a b
1 2 3
a b c d
1 2 3 4 5



#include<stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int i, j;
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        for (j = 0; j <= i; j++)
        {
            if (i % 2)
                printf("%c", 'a' + i);
            else
                printf("%d", 1 + j);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}




 3. Continuous Character Sequence with Case Toggling

Prints letters continuously across rows, toggling case using bit manipulation ('ch ^ 32' toggles the 5th bit — converts uppercase ↔ lowercase):


a B c D e
F g H i
J k L
m N
o


#include<stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int i, j;
    char ch;
    ch = 'a';
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        for (j = 0; j < 5 - i; j++, ch++, ch = ch ^ 32)  // toggles case each character
        {
            printf("%c", ch);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}



 4. Star Pyramid (Half Pyramid, Left-Aligned)

*
* *
* * *
* * * *
* * * * *



#include<stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int i, j;
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        for (j = 0; j <= i; j++)
            printf("* ");
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}



 5. Star Pyramid (Centered / True Triangle) — Using 3 Loops

Uses a triple nested loop: outer loop for rows, first inner loop for leading spaces, second inner loop for stars.


        *
      * *
    * * *
  * * * *
* * * * *



#include<stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int i, j, k;
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        for (j = 0; j < 4 - i; j++)
            printf(" ");
        for (k = 0; k <= i; k++)
            printf("* ");
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}


Space/star count per row:

| Row (i) | Spaces | Stars |

| i = 0   | 4      | 1     |
| i = 1   | 3      | 2     |
| i = 2   | 2      | 3     |
| i = 3   | 1      | 4     |
| i = 4   | 0      | 5     |

> Tip: If a space is printed after each '*' instead of before, the shape becomes a pyramid/triangle ('△') rather than a right-aligned triangle.


 Key Takeaways

- Outer loop (i) → controls rows
- Inner loop (j) → controls columns (usually  j <= i for increasing patterns, j < n - i for decreasing/space patterns)
- Character patterns often use 'a' + j or ch++  with ASCII arithmetic
- Case toggling trick: ch = ch ^ 32  flips between upper and lower case
- Centered/true triangles need a third loop (or two inner loops) — one for leading spaces, one for the actual characters.

